    <h3>Evaluation Pipeline</h3>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>The initial input is a time-stamped dataset consisting of a feature matrix X, labels y, and time-stamps t.</p>
    </div>
    <ol class="steps">
      <li><div><h4>Split in time</h4><p>The dataset is temporally split into a training set and testing months using the TESSERACT framework.</p></div></li>
      <li><div><h4>Rebalance and poison</h4><p>First, the training dataset is rebalanced to a realistic class distribution (e.g., approximately 10% in the case of Android malware). The dataset is then poisoned based on the current iteration’s poisoning rate (starting from 0%, for the hypothetical model baseline) and is then used to train the model.</p></div></li>
      <li><div><h4>Predict</h4><p>The model predicts on the current test month data.</p></div></li>
      <li><div><h4>Sample and retrain</h4><p>After prediction, the passive recovery mechanism (e.g. active learning) selects new samples to be labeled, and then the model is re-trained. The train, predict, sample, and retrain process is then repeated for all test months.</p></div></li>
      <li><div><h4>Repeat and analyse</h4><p>After all test months have been evaluated, the process is repeated for all poisoning and recovery rates in the evaluation settings. Once all setting evaluations have been completed, the intercept and recovery rates are extracted from the results.</p></div></li>
    </ol>

    <h3>Dataset and Classifiers</h3>
    <div class="cards" data-augmented-ui="tl-clip br-clip border">
      <div class="card"><h4>Dataset</h4><p>The dataset considers the popular DREBIN feature representation. We use a feature selection of the top 10,000 features. In the rest of this paper, we refer to this dataset as D1418. The dataset consists of 259,230 applications covering the five-year period of 2014 to 2018. As in related works, we use data from 2014 to train the classifiers.</p></div>
      <div class="card"><h4>Classifiers</h4><p>Linear Support Vector Machines, referred to as SVM, was chosen as it was the classifier initially used in DREBIN. Deep Neural Networks, referred to as DNN, are another common approach for malware classification. We use the same RF settings of 101 decision trees and a maximum depth of 64.</p></div>
    </div>

